\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be an abelian group of order $pq$, with $p$ and $q$ distinct primes. Cauchy's theorem yields elements $a,b\in G$ such that $|a|=p$ and $|b|=q$. Because $G$ is abelian, $a$ and $b$ commute. If $(ab)^n=e$, then $a^n=b^{-n}$. The left side lies in $\langle a\rangle$ and the right side lies in $\langle b\rangle$. These subgroups have coprime orders, so their intersection is trivial; hence $a^n=b^n=e$. Therefore $p\mid n$ and $q\mid n$, so $pq\mid n$. Consequently, $|ab|=pq$. Since $ab$ has order $|G|$, it generates $G$, and $G$ is cyclic.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be an abelian group of order $pq$, where $p$ and $q$ are distinct primes. By Cauchy's theorem, there are elements $a,b\in G$ of orders $p$ and $q$, respectively. Set $c=ab$. Since $G$ is abelian, $a$ and $b$ commute. If $c^n=e$, then $a^n=b^{-n}$. This element belongs to both $\langle a\rangle$ and $\langle b\rangle$. Because these subgroups have coprime orders, their intersection is $\{e\}$, so $a^n=b^n=e$. Thus $p\mid n$ and $q\mid n$, and since $p\ne q$ are primes, $pq\mid n$. It follows that $|c|=pq$. Therefore $\langle c\rangle$ is a subgroup of $G$ with $pq=|G|$ elements, so $\langle c\rangle=G$. Hence $G$ is cyclic.
\end{proof}
